  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m "Logging" is a new subsystem, not an edit to a flow that already exists here — there's
  nothing to change, only scattered [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m calls to replace. So I'll run the full process: questions, approaches, a
  sectioned design, then a written spec — rather than starting to edit [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything after it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the two surfaces in this repo are different runtimes with different logging problems. Browser code can't
  write files and can only reach you by shipping logs over the network; Node code writes to stdout/stderr and gets
  collected by whatever runs it. They don't share an implementation, and "debug production issues" means something
  different in each.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — you're debugging what users hit in the login flow. Buys: visibility into real sessions. Costs: needs
    a transport and a place to send logs, and drags in privacy questions (that username log, the adjacent password
    field). Hard to change later, because the transport is the design.
  - [1mNode only[0m — you're debugging the server/CLI side. Buys: cheapest useful thing; structured output to stdout, no
    transport, no dependency. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBoth, one shared module[0m — a common API with per-runtime sinks. Buys: one vocabulary everywhere. Costs: the shared
    abstraction has to be right before either side is useful, and you pay the browser transport cost regardless.

  [1mMy recommendation: tell me which surface actually has the production issues[0m, and if it's genuinely both, I'd still
  start with Node-only and add the browser sink as a second piece — the browser half carries all of the risk and none of
  the two is blocked by the other.
